+{"event_type":"assistant_text","timestamp":"2026-09-15T23:59:09.681Z","session_id":null,"call_id":null,"payload":{"event":{"id":"evt_0a7829b70001x5yNNTYRKzXP6W","type":"message.part.updated","properties":{"sessionID":"ses_f59d1c961ffe3epkVD17Dploo4","part":{"id":"prt_0a78295ed001aTQOu0iyeeZIw5","messageID":"msg_0a78273a5001fVz9QbsQxgCKbX","sessionID":"ses_f59d1c961ffe3epkVD17Dploo4","type":"text","text":"That is a bug in the plugin’s event classification.

The plugin currently labels every text `message.part.updated` event as `assistant_text`:

```js
if (partType !== "reasoning" && partType !== "text") return

await writeEvent(partType === "reasoning" ? "reasoning" : "assistant_text", ...)
```

OpenCode emits text parts for both user and assistant messages. Therefore the user’s prompt can appear as `assistant_text`, especially when:

```text
payload.event.properties.part.messageID == payload.prompt_id
```

The duplicate rows are likely repeated updates to the same message part. The event stream reports snapshots/updates, not necessarily one unique row per final text value.

You can identify these as user text with:

```python
def part(payload):
    return payload.get("event", {}).get("properties", {}).get("part", {})

telemetry["part_message_id"] = telemetry["payload"].apply(
    lambda p: part(p).get("messageID")
)

telemetry["is_prompt_text"] = (
    (telemetry["event_type"] == "assistant_text") &
    (telemetry["part_message_id"] == telemetry["prompt_id"])
)
```

So those rows are probably the user prompt being mislabeled, not the assistant repeating it. The plugin should classify text based on message role or message ID and ideally deduplicate repeated part updates.","time":{"start":1789516748269,"end":1789516749680},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_0a31daffcaf06be9016aa9dbcc0d7887d2b0a6b41cca105f6f","phase":"final_answer"}}},"time":1789516749680}},"prompt_id":"msg_0a7827397001SEM6yHlH7we13C"}}
